{
  "issue": 31,
  "version": 2,
  "understanding": "The previous plan was wrong about the SHAPE of the problem, and that is why two bugs survived a PR that passed all ten of its own acceptance criteria.\n\nIt treated \"report rather than throw\" as an inventory of five call sites instead of as a missing error boundary. Its approach item (2) says it wrapped `git fetch` in a try/catch, and its risks list says \"The fetch-failure catch is an addition beyond the nine items in the ticket\" — so it appended a sixth guarded call site to a list rather than putting a boundary under the pipeline. The `git diff` on the very next statement is unguarded, so the contract the PR's own new comment asserts (\"This function's contract is to report rather than throw\") is false one line below the guard the PR added. QA found this as BUG-1. I confirmed it: `git fetch origin qa-tag-1` exits 0 in the sandbox and the `git diff` on the next line exits 128.\n\nThe plan made it unrecoverable by writing \"A unit-test harness for bin/release-notes-check.mjs\" into out_of_scope, on the grounds that the script is module-level and side-effecting. So the file under repair had ZERO automated coverage: packages/ci-actions/vitest.config.mts has include: ['src/**/*.test.(js|jsx|ts|tsx)'] and the gate lives in bin/. Every fix in that PR was verified only by a human hand-running the script, which is why the surviving defects are exactly the two spots just outside the five the plan enumerated, and why nothing in CI could have caught them.\n\nI found a third instance QA did not, and it is the evidence that per-call-site work cannot converge on this file. `reportError` — the function the PR turned into the single funnel — is itself not total: its own `fs.readFileSync(readmePath, 'utf-8')` throws EISDIR when a PR replaces `upcoming-release-notes/README.md` with a directory. Reproduced at the PR commit: the `::error::` line IS printed (1 line, 99 bytes) and zero `::notice::` lines, and then the process dies on a raw `Error: EISDIR: illegal operation on a directory, read` stack with 5 frames before it can set its exit code. The error reporter is an error path. Widening the fetch try to cover `git diff`, which is what QA's hint proposes, does not touch this.\n\nOne of BUG-1's two triggers is also not a failure that should merely be reported. The 1MB maxBuffer overflow: I built the branch QA describes (8000 notes with 180-character filenames, 1728000 bytes of diff output) and measured it. Catching it and printing \"git diff failed\" would be wrong — 8000 valid notes is a branch that ought to pass. Raising maxBuffer makes it exit 0 with \"Validated 8000 release note(s)\". I verified that, plus BUG-1(a), BUG-2 and the EISDIR case, against a real bare-remote sandbox at the PR commit and then against the fix.\n\nON THE REJECTED REVISION. The plan reviewer refused the previous root-cause output on one point and I accept it: AC-15 stated the rule \"each case fails when its guard is removed\" and then gave the worked example \"remove the top-level catch and confirm the git-command case fails\" — but nothing in the proposed failures.test.js imports or spawns the bin script, so removing `void main().catch(...)` leaves all five cases green. The criterion claimed coverage the suite did not have, and its own verification procedure followed literally would have reported success on a suite that does not test the boundary at all. The reviewer's option (a) is correct and this revision takes it: a sixth case spawns bin/release-notes-check.mjs with spawnSync, cwd set to a bare-remote sandbox, BASE_REF set to a ref git fetches but cannot resolve as origin/<ref>. I built and ran that exact spawn call by hand against the extracted PR script and got `::error::Release notes check failed: git diff failed with exit 128: fatal: bad revision 'origin/qa-tag-1...HEAD'`, exit 1, stderr 0 bytes. I then removed the boundary from the patched copy and re-ran the same call: 0 ::error:: lines and 6 stderr frames. The case therefore fails when the guard is removed, which is what AC-15 now says and what it can now be checked against. Everything else from the refused revision stands; the correction is additive (a sixth test case, and AC-15 rewritten to match the suite that will exist) rather than a restart.",
  "root_cause": "The gate's pipeline has no error boundary, and its reporting function is not itself total. Every throw escapes the `void (async () => { ... })()` IIFE as an unhandled rejection and terminates Node with a stack trace on stderr and zero `::error::` lines on stdout — for a gate whose entire job is to write an annotation. The plan's fix was applied per call site, so each guard left its immediate neighbours exposed; the third instance proves it, because it lives inside `reportError` itself, downstream of every guard the PR added. Contributing and separately fixable: `promisify(execFile)` applies a 1MB maxBuffer to `git diff`, which is a ceiling on legitimate branch size rather than a failure at all.",
  "approach": "Put one boundary under the pipeline, and move the three shapes of failure into small total functions that vitest can finally reach. This is deliberately NOT QA's suggested one-liner and NOT a re-run of the previous plan's approach.\n\nNEW FILE `packages/ci-actions/src/release-notes/failures.mjs`, three exported functions, each total over one failure shape:\n  1. `describeFailure(error)` — returns ONE line, never a stack. Recognise an `execFile` rejection by its own `cmd`/`code`/`stderr` properties and render `git <subcommand> failed with exit <code>: <git stderr flattened to one line>`; that names the failing command and the offending revision, which a raw `Command failed: git diff --name-status -z --diff-filter=AMR ...` dump does not. Fall back to `error.message` / `error.name`, and to `String(error)` for a non-Error throw, so it cannot itself throw. Verified shape: `git diff failed with exit 128: fatal: bad revision 'origin/qa-tag-1...HEAD'`.\n  2. `readReadme(readmePath)` — returns the file's text or `''`, catching EVERY throw. This subsumes the existing `fs.existsSync` pre-check, so that double lookup goes away rather than being wrapped twice.\n  3. `parseNote(path)` — returns `{ ok: true, data, content }` or `{ ok: false, message }`, wrapping BOTH `readFileSync` and `matter()`. This is where BUG-2 is caught, and it is caught HERE rather than only at the boundary so the message can name the note: `Release note <label> could not be parsed: missed comma between flow collection entries at line 3, column 1`. I verified this matters: with the boundary in place but parseNote removed, BUG-2 produces `Release notes check failed: missed comma between flow collection entries...` — exit 1 and no stack, but nothing telling the contributor which file is theirs. The boundary alone is a regression detector here; parseNote is what makes the message usable.\n\nPut them in `src/` beside `util.mjs`, which `bin/release-notes-check.mjs` already imports from. That is what makes them testable at all — `src/**/*.test.*` is vitest's include, so this is the only way the file under repair acquires coverage without restructuring the gate.\n\nEDIT `packages/ci-actions/bin/release-notes-check.mjs`, six edits:\n  (a) import the three helpers.\n  (b) `reportError` calls `readReadme` in place of its inline `existsSync`/`readFileSync` pair. `reportError` becomes total, so the EISDIR case reports instead of crashing.\n  (c) `validateFile` calls `parseNote`; on `!ok` it calls `reportError` with the label and the parse message and returns false. All SEVEN of its existing reportError messages keep their exact current text — only the parse gains a guard. Grep for them; do not trust a count.\n  (d) DROP `import matter from 'gray-matter';` from the bin script. After (c) it has no remaining reference, and `.oxlintrc.json` sets `eslint/no-unused-vars: error`, so leaving it fails `yarn lint`. `fs` stays — it is still used by `createWriteStream` and `existsSync`.\n  (e) Rename the IIFE to `async function main() { ... }` and end the file with `void main().catch(e => reportError(`Release notes check failed: ${describeFailure(e)}`));`. This is the boundary. It covers the `git diff` call (BUG-1a), the maxBuffer residue, and anything a future edit adds.\n  (f) KEEP the per-call `try/catch` around `git fetch` exactly as PR #32 has it. Removing it would be a re-roll; it is correct, it produces the message QA has already verified against AC-10, and the boundary sits under it without changing it. Add `{ maxBuffer: 64 * 1024 * 1024 }` alongside the existing `{ encoding: 'latin1' }` on the diff call. Comment why: ~64x Node's 1MB default and ~38x the 1728000 bytes I measured, so no human branch reaches it, and if one ever did the boundary reports it as an annotation rather than a stack.\n\nNEW FILE `packages/ci-actions/src/release-notes/failures.test.js`, SIX cases. Five unit cases against the real filesystem via `fs.mkdtempSync(os.tmpdir())` — no stubbing, so the EISDIR case is a genuine reproduction rather than a mock agreeing with itself: a thrown execFile rejection renders one line naming the command; `describeFailure` handles a plain Error and a non-Error throw; a README that is a directory yields `''`; a note with `category: [unclosed` yields `ok: false` carrying the YAML reason; a well-formed note passes through unchanged, so the catch cannot silently swallow a valid note.\n\nThe sixth is the correction the plan reviewer asked for, and it is what makes AC-15 true: spawn the real `bin/release-notes-check.mjs` with `child_process.spawnSync(process.execPath, [scriptPath], { cwd: sandbox, env: { ...process.env, BASE_REF: 'qa-tag-1', GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY: undefined }, encoding: 'utf-8' })` and assert exit 1, at least one line starting `::error::`, and zero bytes on stderr. Build the sandbox the way the reviewer's finding describes — `fs.mkdtempSync` a directory, `git init --bare` a remote, clone it, seed `upcoming-release-notes/README.md` plus a valid note on a `base` branch, branch, commit a valid note, then `git tag qa-tag-1 && git push origin qa-tag-1`. Set GIT_AUTHOR_*/GIT_COMMITTER_* in the spawn env: CI has no global git identity, and the commit fails without them. Derive scriptPath from `import.meta.url` (`new URL('../../bin/release-notes-check.mjs', import.meta.url)`) so it does not depend on the runner's cwd. I ran this spawn call by hand against the extracted PR script and it produced exactly `::error::Release notes check failed: git diff failed with exit 128: fatal: bad revision 'origin/qa-tag-1...HEAD'`, exit 1, stderr 0 bytes; removing the boundary and re-running gave 0 ::error:: lines and 6 stderr frames, so the assertion genuinely fails without the guard. Do NOT skip the git setup on grounds of speed — it is a handful of local commands and it is the only thing in the suite that exercises the boundary.\n\nPut the new tests in a NEW file rather than extending `util.test.js` on purpose: AC-7 asserts `util.test.js reports 33 passing`, and keeping them separate means that criterion stays literally true rather than being quietly rewritten.\n\nPlus one release note.\n\nI applied all of the above to a copy of the PR's script and ran every case against a real bare-remote sandbox. Results: BUG-1(a) `::error::Release notes check failed: git diff failed with exit 128: fatal: bad revision 'origin/qa-tag-1...HEAD'`, exit 1, stderr 0 bytes; the 1728000-byte diff `Validated 8000 release note(s). 🎉`, exit 0; BUG-2 `::error::Release note upcoming-release-notes/broken.md could not be parsed: missed comma between flow collection entries at line 3, column 1`, exit 1, stderr 0 bytes; the EISDIR case exit 1, 1 `::error::`, 1 `::notice::` (empty), stderr 0 bytes; and a valid note still exits 0 with `Validated 1 release note(s). 🎉`.",
  "files": [
    {
      "path": "packages/ci-actions/src/release-notes/failures.mjs",
      "action": "create",
      "change": "Three exported total functions: `describeFailure(error)` returning one line for an ::error:: annotation (recognising an execFile rejection by its cmd/code/stderr and naming the git subcommand and exit code, falling back to error.message/error.name then String(error)); `readReadme(readmePath)` returning the file's text or '' on any throw; `parseNote(path)` returning { ok: true, data, content } or { ok: false, message }, wrapping both readFileSync and matter(). Module doc comment recording the invariant that none of the three may throw, since each sits on the failure path.",
      "why": "Put the failure handling in src/, which vitest covers, instead of bin/, which nothing covers. This is what turns 'wrap two more call sites' into a boundary that CI can hold in place."
    },
    {
      "path": "packages/ci-actions/bin/release-notes-check.mjs",
      "action": "edit",
      "change": "Import the three helpers. Drop the now-unused `import matter from 'gray-matter'` (no-unused-vars is an error in .oxlintrc.json, so leaving it fails lint; `fs` is still needed). reportError uses readReadme in place of its inline existsSync/readFileSync pair, making the error reporter total. validateFile calls parseNote and, on !ok, calls reportError with `Release note <label> could not be parsed: <message>` and returns false, leaving its seven existing messages byte-identical. Rename the `void (async () => {...})()` to `async function main()` and terminate the file with `void main().catch(e => reportError(`Release notes check failed: ${describeFailure(e)}`));`. Add `maxBuffer: 64 * 1024 * 1024` to the git diff call's existing options object, with a comment saying it is a deliberate widening over Node's 1MB default and that the boundary catches the residue. KEEP the existing per-call try/catch around `git fetch` exactly as it stands.",
      "why": "Holds BUG-1 (both triggers) and BUG-2, and closes the EISDIR instance inside reportError. Retaining the fetch guard is deliberate: it is already verified by AC-10 and replacing it would discard passing work."
    },
    {
      "path": "packages/ci-actions/src/release-notes/failures.test.js",
      "action": "create",
      "change": "Six cases. Five unit cases under a temp directory made with fs.mkdtempSync: describeFailure on an execFile rejection naming the command; describeFailure on a plain Error and on a non-Error throw; readReadme on a path that is a directory returning ''; parseNote on front matter `category: [unclosed` returning ok: false with the YAML reason; parseNote on a well-formed note returning ok: true with its data, so the new catch cannot swallow a valid release note. Plus a sixth case that spawns the real bin/release-notes-check.mjs with spawnSync — cwd set to a fs.mkdtempSync sandbox holding a bare remote, a `base` branch and a pushed tag `qa-tag-1`, GIT_AUTHOR_*/GIT_COMMITTER_* set in the env, BASE_REF=qa-tag-1, GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY removed — asserting exit 1, at least one line starting `::error::`, and zero bytes on stderr.",
      "why": "The gate in bin/ has no coverage today, which is why its perimeter rotted unnoticed. The spawn case is the one that makes AC-15 true: the five unit cases never load the bin script, so without it a removed top-level catch would leave the suite green."
    },
    {
      "path": "upcoming-release-notes/fix-release-notes-gate-error-boundary.md",
      "action": "create",
      "change": "Release note, category Bugfix, authors [Shivam-Fl], single-line body stating that the check reports a bad base ref, unreadable README or malformed note front matter as an annotation instead of a Node stack trace, and validates branches whose release-note diff is large. No slug collision with fix-release-notes-gate-crashes.md or fix-release-notes-gate-io-paths.md.",
      "why": "The gate this change hardens is what enforces the note, and the note must itself pass it."
    }
  ],
  "tests": [
    {
      "path": "packages/ci-actions/src/release-notes/failures.test.js",
      "action": "create",
      "cases": [
        "describeFailure turns an execFile rejection into one line naming the git subcommand and its exit code, never the raw 'Command failed' dump (reproduces BUG-1: without it the gate's ::error:: line is a 160-character command echo)",
        "describeFailure returns error.message for a plain Error and String(error) for a non-Error throw, so the boundary's own formatter cannot become the next crash",
        "readReadme returns an empty string when the path is a directory instead of propagating EISDIR (reproduces the third instance: reportError crashed on a PR that replaces README.md with a directory)",
        "parseNote returns ok:false carrying the YAML reason for front matter `category: [unclosed` instead of throwing a YAMLException out of the pipeline (reproduces BUG-2)",
        "parseNote returns ok:true and the parsed data for a well-formed note, so the new catch cannot swallow a valid release note",
        "spawning bin/release-notes-check.mjs with BASE_REF set to a pushed tag prints an ::error:: line naming the diff failure, exits 1 and writes nothing to stderr (reproduces BUG-1 at the boundary itself, and fails if `void main().catch(...)` is removed — measured: with the boundary 1 ::error:: line and 0 stderr frames, without it 0 ::error:: lines and 6 stderr frames)"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "acceptance": [
    {
      "id": "AC-1",
      "check": "A branch deleting upcoming-release-notes/ entirely makes the gate print '::error::No release note added under upcoming-release-notes/.' and exit 1, with no ENOENT, stack trace or 'Unhandled' output.",
      "how_to_verify": "Sandbox with a real git remote; git rm -r the directory, commit, run with BASE_REF=base and GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY set. grep -c ENOENT = 0. I measured exactly this output; before the fix it was an ENOENT stack trace.",
      "verify": "test"
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-2",
      "check": "With GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY pointing into a nonexistent directory, the gate prints its ::error:: and ::notice:: lines and exits 1 without an unhandled 'error' event.",
      "how_to_verify": "Run with GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY=/tmp/nope/deep/s.md after rm -rf /tmp/nope; expect grep -c Unhandled = 0 and exit 1. I measured Unhandled = 1 before the fix and 0 after, exit 1 both times.",
      "verify": "test"
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-3",
      "check": "With GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY unset and a README exceeding the 64KB pipe buffer, the full output reaches the pipe instead of stopping near 65536 bytes.",
      "how_to_verify": "200000-byte README piped to wc -c; expect above 190000. I measured 200113 after the fix, 65688 before.",
      "verify": "test"
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-4",
      "check": "A long ::error:: is not truncated: a note crediting 20000 bot authors emits the whole author list rather than cutting mid-author.",
      "how_to_verify": "wc -c on the unset-summary path; expect above 150000. I measured 160128 after the fix, 65586 before.",
      "verify": "test"
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-5",
      "check": "A note whose filename holds a non-UTF-8 byte is selected and validated (exit 0), and with an invalid category is rejected naming the file rather than 'does not exist on HEAD'.",
      "how_to_verify": "Create the file with raw 0xE9 bytes via python, commit, run with BASE_REF=base. I measured 'Validated 1 release note(s) 🎉' exit 0 after the fix, and a false 'does not exist on HEAD' before it.",
      "verify": "test"
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-6",
      "check": "A valid UTF-8 filename (café.md) still validates and still appears correctly in an error message — the latin1 decode must not mangle names the old code handled.",
      "how_to_verify": "Commit café.md valid, then with an invalid category; confirm the ::error:: shows 'upcoming-release-notes/café.md' with no replacement characters. I verified both, exit 0 and exit 1 respectively.",
      "verify": "test"
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-7",
      "check": "A C row does not desynchronise the row after it: given a C100 row with two paths followed by an A row, only real.md is reported as added and changed.",
      "how_to_verify": "Run `yarn workspace @actual-app/ci-actions run test`; expect 33 passing in util.test.js. To prove the case is falsifiable, temporarily delete `'C'` from hasTwoPaths in util.mjs, confirm exactly that case fails and the other 32 pass, then restore and re-run for 33/33. The new tests from this attempt live in failures.test.js, so util.test.js stays at 33 and this criterion does not need rewriting.",
      "verify": "test"
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-8",
      "check": "Ordinary verdicts are unchanged: valid note exits 0, invalid category produces the same ::error:: text, a rename validates the new path, no note added exits 1, and a deleted README still reports its category error with an empty ::notice::. Output is identical to the unmodified script apart from the removed 'Looking in' debug line.",
      "how_to_verify": "Five sandbox branches diffed against the same branches run on the unmodified script, with the deleted debug line filtered out of both sides. Re-run it against the PR-32 commit as the baseline, not against origin/main, since origin/main is two changes behind.",
      "verify": "test"
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-9",
      "check": "Exit code is 1 on every error path, including the two that previously exited before draining and the newly caught fetch failure — a truncated or crashed message must never come with exit 0.",
      "how_to_verify": "echo $? after each AC-2/3/4/8 run, and after the AC-10 run.",
      "verify": "test"
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-10",
      "check": "A base ref that cannot be fetched produces a ::error:: line naming the ref and exits 1, rather than an unhandled promise rejection with a raw stack trace.",
      "how_to_verify": "Run with BASE_REF=nonexistent-branch against the sandbox remote. Expect a ::error:: line mentioning the ref and exit 1, and no 'Command failed' stack trace reaching the log uncaught.",
      "verify": "test"
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-11",
      "check": "A base ref that git can fetch but cannot resolve as origin/<ref> — a tag is enough — produces an ::error:: line naming that ref and exits 1, instead of dying on `fatal: bad revision` with an empty stdout.",
      "how_to_verify": "BUG-1(a). Push a tag to the sandbox remote, commit a valid note, run with BASE_REF=qa-tag-1. Expect at least one line starting `::error::` containing the string 'qa-tag-1', exit 1, and zero bytes on stderr. Confirm `git fetch origin qa-tag-1` exits 0 first, so the test is really exercising the diff and not the fetch. I reproduced the current failure (stdout 0 bytes, 0 ::error:: lines, 6-frame stack) and measured the fixed behaviour against my own patch: 1 ::error:: line, exit 1, stderr 0 bytes.",
      "verify": "test"
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-12",
      "check": "A branch adding enough release notes for `git diff --name-status -z` to exceed 1MB of output is validated, not crashed: the gate exits 0 and reports the number of notes it checked.",
      "how_to_verify": "BUG-1(b). Commit 8000 notes with ~200-byte filenames so the diff output is over 1MB — I measured 1728000 bytes. Expect exit 0 and `Validated 8000 release note(s)`, and zero bytes on stderr. An ::error:: line here would mean the diff was caught but not accommodated, which is still wrong: the branch is legitimate.",
      "verify": "test"
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-13",
      "check": "A release note whose YAML front matter is malformed — an unterminated flow sequence such as `category: [unclosed` — is reported the way every other invalid note is: one ::error:: line naming that file and what is wrong with it, exit 1, and nothing on stderr.",
      "how_to_verify": "BUG-2. printf the broken front matter into upcoming-release-notes/broken.md, commit, run with BASE_REF=base. Expect an ::error:: line containing 'broken.md' and the YAML reason, exit 1, and zero bytes on stderr. I reproduced the current 10-frame YAMLException and measured the fixed message against my own patch. The 'containing broken.md' part is load-bearing: with only the boundary and no per-file parse guard the line is `Release notes check failed: missed comma...` and names nothing.",
      "verify": "test"
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-14",
      "check": "No state a PR author can put in upcoming-release-notes/ stops the gate from finishing its own report: a branch that replaces upcoming-release-notes/README.md with a directory still gets its ::error:: and ::notice:: lines printed and exits 1, rather than dying mid-report on an EISDIR.",
      "how_to_verify": "The instance QA did not find, and the reason per-call-site fixing cannot converge — the error reporter was itself an error path. Replace README.md with a directory holding a file, add a note with an invalid category, commit, run with BASE_REF=base. Expect a non-empty ::error:: line, an ::notice:: line present, exit 1, and zero bytes on stderr. I measured today's failure exactly: 1 ::error:: line and 0 ::notice:: lines, then a 5-frame EISDIR stack; after the fix, 1 ::error:: and 1 ::notice:: (empty), exit 1, stderr 0 bytes.",
      "verify": "test"
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-15",
      "check": "The gate's failure paths are covered by the package test suite rather than only by hand-running the script: `yarn workspace @actual-app/ci-actions run test` covers the unreadable-README and unparseable-note cases as unit tests of failures.mjs, and covers the failed-git-command case by spawning bin/release-notes-check.mjs itself, so each case fails when the guard it covers is removed.",
      "how_to_verify": "This is the permanence mechanism, and it is the criterion the previous attempt lacked. It is now true, and this is the check that establishes it. Run the suite and confirm all six cases in failures.test.js execute. Then falsify each guard in turn. Remove `void main().catch(...)`: the spawned-gate case must fail (I measured 0 ::error:: lines and 6 stderr frames without it, against 1 ::error:: line and 0 stderr frames with it). Restore it, and remove the parseNote guard in validateFile, replacing it with the bare `matter(fs.readFileSync(path, 'utf-8'))` the PR had: the unparseable-note case must fail on the missing filename, because the boundary alone emits `Release notes check failed: missed comma...` and names no file. Restore both and confirm green. A run that stays green after deleting the guards proves nothing — that is exactly what the previous revision of this criterion would have reported.",
      "verify": "test"
    }
  ],
  "qa_script": [
    "Sandbox setup (unchanged, and it still resolves BASE_REF=base exactly as Actions does): bare repo, a clone whose origin is that bare repo, a `base` branch holding upcoming-release-notes/README.md plus a seed note. Set GIT_AUTHOR_*/GIT_COMMITTER_*; commit messages must start with [AI] or the repo's git guard rejects them. Never `git add -A` from inside the outer repo — stage explicit paths.",
    "Baseline for AC-8 is the PR-32 commit, not origin/main: extract that commit's release-notes-check.mjs, repoint its util import, and run the five ordinary branches against both scripts, filtering the removed 'Looking in' line from both sides.",
    "AC-1: branch off base, `git rm -r upcoming-release-notes`, commit. BASE_REF=base, GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY set. exit 1, the ::error:: line, grep -c ENOENT = 0.",
    "AC-2: on a branch with `category: Nonsense`, `rm -rf /tmp/nope`, GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY=/tmp/nope/deep/s.md. exit 1, both message lines, grep -c Unhandled = 0.",
    "AC-3: 200000-byte README committed, `env -u GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY ... | wc -c` — expect above 190000.",
    "AC-4: a note whose authors list is 20000 comma-separated claude entries; same piped byte count — expect above 150000.",
    "AC-5: create the note with raw 0xE9 bytes via python, commit. Expect 'Validated 1 release note(s) 🎉' exit 0; then rewrite with `category: Nonsense` and confirm the error names the file and never says 'does not exist on HEAD'.",
    "AC-6: café.md valid — exit 0; then `category: Nonsense` — the ::error:: shows 'upcoming-release-notes/café.md' with no replacement characters.",
    "AC-7: run the package suite; util.test.js reports 33. Falsifiability: delete `'C'` from hasTwoPaths in util.mjs, confirm exactly that one case fails, restore, re-run.",
    "AC-8: five regression branches off base (valid note; `category: Nonsense`; `git mv good.md renamed.md`; no note added; README deleted plus a Nonsense note), each with BASE_REF=base and GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY set. cmp both scripts' stdout and exit codes after filtering the debug line.",
    "AC-9: echo $? immediately after every AC-2/3/4/8/10/11/12/13/14 run and confirm the stated code.",
